{"id":"neet-2026-q010","pn":10,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Nuclei","st":"Nuclear size and density","q":"An unknown nucleus has a nuclear density of 2.29×10¹⁷ kg/m³ and mass of 19.926×10⁻²⁷ kg.. Its mass number A is approximately:\n(Take R₀ = 1.2×10⁻¹⁵ m, 4π = 12.56)","o":["12","19","20","16"],"c":0,"s":"Given: ρ = 2.29×10¹⁷ kg/m³, mass m = 19.926×10⁻²⁷ kg, R₀ = 1.2×10⁻¹⁵ m and R = R₀A^(1/3).\nVolume = Mass/Density, so (4/3)πR³ = M/ρ.\n(4/3)π[R₀A^(1/3)]³ = M/ρ.\n(4/3)πR₀³A = M/ρ.\nA = (M/ρ) × 3/(4π R₀³).\nA = (19.926×10⁻²⁷ × 3)/(2.29×10¹⁷ × 12.56 × (1.2×10⁻¹⁵)³) ≈ 12."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q036","pn":36,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Nuclei","st":"Q value of nuclear reaction","q":"Consider the following nuclear reaction\n²³⁸U → ²³⁴Th + ⁴He\nTake masses of ²³⁸U, ²³⁴Th and ⁴He as 238.050 u, 234.043 u and 4.003 u, respectively. The Q value for the reaction, in keV, is:\n[Given: 1 u = 931.5 MeV c⁻²]","o":["3730","3736","3740","3726"],"c":3,"s":"Q-value of a nuclear reaction is Q = Δmc².\nMass defect Δm = m(reactants) − m(products).\nΔm = 238.050 − (234.043 + 4.003) = 0.004 u.\nQ = 0.004 × 931.5 MeV = 3.726 MeV = 3726 keV."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q037","pn":37,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Nuclei","st":"Nuclear size and mass defect","q":"Four statements are given (A is mass number):\nA. The volume of a nucleus is proportional to A^(1/3).\nB. The volume of a nucleus is proportional to A.\nC. The difference in mass of an atom and its nucleus is called the mass defect.\nD. The difference in mass of a nucleus and its constituents is called the mass defect.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["B and D are true, but A and C are false","A and D are true, but B and C are false","A and C are true, but B and D are false","B and C are true, but A and D are false"],"c":0,"s":"Size of nucleus: r = r₀A^(1/3).\nCubing: r³ = r₀³·A.\nVolume V = (4/3)πr³, so V ∝ A.\nThus A is wrong and B is correct.\nThe difference between the actual mass of the nucleus and its constituents is the mass defect.\nSo C is wrong and D is correct."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q021","pn":21,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Nuclei","st":"Alpha, beta, positron decay","q":"²⁹⁰₈₂X →(α) Y →(e⁺) Z →(β⁻) P →(e⁻) Q\nIn the nuclear emission stated above, the mass number and atomic number of the product Q respectively, are:","o":["288, 82","286, 81","280, 81","286, 80"],"c":1,"s":"α emission lowers A by 4 and Z by 2: ²⁹⁰₈₂X → ²⁸⁶₈₀Y.\nPositron (e⁺) emission keeps A and lowers Z by 1: ²⁸⁶₇₉Z.\nβ⁻ emission keeps A and raises Z by 1: ²⁸⁶₈₀P.\nElectron (e⁻) emission again raises Z by 1: ²⁸⁶₈₁Q.\nSo A = 286 and Z = 81."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q002","pn":2,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Nuclei","st":"Radioactive decay and half-life","q":"The half life of a radioactive substance is 20 minutes. In how much time, the activity of substance drops to (1/16)th of its of its initial value?","o":["80 minutes","20 minutes","40 minutes","60 minutes"],"c":0,"s":"After n half-lives, activity A = A₀/2ⁿ.\nGiven A/A₀ = 1/16 = 1/2⁴, so n = 4.\nn = t/T½, so t = 4 × T½ = 4 × 20.\nt = 80 minutes."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q010","pn":10,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Nuclei","st":"Beta plus decay","q":"In the given nuclear reaction, the element X is\n²²₁₁Na → X + e⁺ + ν","o":["²³₁₀Ne","²²₁₀Ne","²²₁₂Mg","²³₁₁Na"],"c":1,"s":"Write the reaction as ²²₁₁Na → ᴬZX + ⁰₊₁e + ν.\nCharge conservation: 11 = Z + 1, so Z = 10, which is Ne.\nMass number: 22 = A + 0, so A = 22.\nX is ²²₁₀Ne."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q027","pn":27,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Nuclei","st":"Radioactive decay law","q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: The law of radioactive decay states that the number of nuclei undergoing the decay per unit time is inversely proportional to the total number of nuclei in the sample.\nStatement II: The half life of a radionuclide is the sum of the life time of all nuclei, divided by the initial concentration of the nuclei at the t = 0.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","o":["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"c":2,"s":"The decay law says −dN/dt ∝ N, so the rate is directly proportional to N, not inversely. Statement I is wrong.\nHalf life is the time in which half of the active nuclei decay. Statement II describes mean life, not half life, so it is wrong too."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q036","pn":36,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Nuclei","st":"Nuclear size and mass number","q":"A nucleus of mass number 189 splits into two nuclei having mass number 125 and 64. The ratio of radius of two daughter nuclei respectively is","o":["4 : 5","5 : 4","25 : 16","1 : 1"],"c":1,"s":"Nuclear radius varies as R = R₀A^(1/3).\nR₁/R₂ = (125/64)^(1/3).\nThis is 5/4, so the ratio is 5 : 4."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q040","pn":40,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Nuclei","st":"Decay of two samples","q":"At any instant, two elements X₁ and X₂ have same number of radioactive atoms. If the decay constant of X₁ and X₂ are 10λ and λ respectively, then the time when the ratio of their atoms becomes 1/e respectively will be:","o":["1/(5λ)","1/(11λ)","1/(9λ)","1/(6λ)"],"c":2,"s":"N_x = N₀e^(−λₓt) and N_y = N₀e^(−λᵧt).\nN_x/N_y = e^(−(λₓ−λᵧ)t) = 1/e = e⁻¹.\nSo (λₓ − λᵧ)t = 1.\nt = 1/(10λ − λ) = 1/(9λ)."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q014","pn":14,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Nuclei","st":"Radioactive decay","q":"A radioactive nucleus ᴬ_Z X undergoes spontaneous decay in the sequence ᴬ_Z X → _{Z−1}B → _{Z−3}C → _{Z−2}D, where Z is the atomic number of element X. The possible decay particles in the sequence are","o":["β⁻, α, β⁺","α, β⁻, β⁺","α, β⁺, β⁻","β⁺, α, β⁻"],"c":3,"s":"Z → Z–1: atomic number falls by 1, so β⁺.\nZ–1 → Z–3: falls by 2, so α.\nZ–3 → Z–2: rises by 1, so β⁻.\nOrder: β⁺, α, β⁻."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q019","pn":19,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Nuclei","st":"Half-life","q":"The half-life of a radioactive nuclide is 100 hours. The fraction of original activity that will remain after 150 hours would be -","o":["2/(3√2)","1/2","1/(2√2)","2/3"],"c":2,"s":"t/T½ = 150/100 = 3/2.\nA/A₀ = (1/2)^(3/2) = 1/(2√2)."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q022","pn":22,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Nuclei","st":"Binding energy","q":"A nucleus with mass number 240 breaks into two fragments each of mass number 120, the binding energy per nucleon of unfragmented nuclei is 7.6 MeV while that of fragments is 8.5 MeV. The total gain in the Binding Energy in the process is","o":["216 MeV","0.9 MeV","9.4 MeV","804 MeV"],"c":0,"s":"Binding energy before = 240 × 7.6 = 1824 MeV.\nBinding energy after = 240 × 8.5 = 2040 MeV.\nGain = 2040 – 1824 = 216 MeV."}
